# 10. Final focused recommendation

I would implement the following target variants, in order:

1. **Raw:**
   [
   y_i=\log S_i.
   ]

2. **Strict validation:**
   [
   y_i=\log S_i,\quad i\in\mathcal V.
   ]

3. **Weighted validation:**
   [
   \min_f\sum_i q_i\ell(y_i,f(X_i)).
   ]

4. **Robust noisy-sale loss:**
   Huber, MAE, Student-(t), or interval loss.

5. **Time-adjusted target:**
   [
   y_i+\widehat\tau(T,t_i).
   ]

6. **Local market-belief shrinkage target:**
   [
   \alpha_i y_i+(1-\alpha_i)\mu_i^{loc}.
   ]

7. **Robust shrinkage target:**
   shrinkage target plus robust loss or interval uncertainty.

Then compare each against the spatial-feature approach:

[
\hat y_i=f(X_i,\mu_i^{loc}).
]

The strongest final candidate will likely be one of these two:

[
\boxed{
\text{Raw or lightly time-adjusted target}
+
\text{scaled-time spatial comparable feature}
+
\text{equity calibration}
}
]

or

[
\boxed{
\text{mild shrinkage target}
+
\text{full AVM on }X
+
\text{equity calibration}
}
]

I would not jump directly to aggressive target smoothing. Start with (\alpha\ge 0.75), then test whether lower (\alpha) actually improves ratio curves without hurting unique-property accuracy.

